# The Firebase web config that backs the whole app: Firestore, Auth and the callables. These are NOT secrets - a
# Firebase web config is public by design, and the project is protected by security rules and App Check. For local dev,
# copy this file to .env and fill the values in; for GitHub Pages, set them as repository secrets of the same names.
#
# There is no VITE_APPS_SCRIPT_URL any more. The Google Sheet is no longer a backend for anything: every read and write
# goes to Firestore, the login is Firebase's, and the deployed script is kept only as a reference for what the app used
# to do. See the data-model section of the README.
#
# The values come from the Firebase console: Project settings > Your apps > Web app > SDK setup and configuration.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
# The default Cloud Storage bucket, from the same SDK snippet - it looks like fire-clock-76723.firebasestorage.app for a
# project created after September 2024, or <project>.appspot.com for an older one. PUBLIC like the rest of the web config
# above: a bucket name is not a secret, and storage.rules is what protects what is inside it.
#
# It is used by ONE feature - the scan attached to a certification - so a build without it still signs in and works. What
# it cannot do is upload or open a scan, and the attachment section says so rather than failing at the first byte.
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# The FCM web-push certificate key, from Project settings > Cloud Messaging > Web configuration.
VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY=
# The Google Analytics measurement id for this web app (Project settings > Integrations > Google Analytics, or the web
# app's own SDK snippet - it looks like G-XXXXXXXXXX). PUBLIC by design, like the rest of the web config above.
#
# Leave it EMPTY and nothing is reported at all: Analytics is initialised only when an id is present, so switching it on
# is a deploy rather than a migration, and a build without one behaves exactly as it did before. A loopback origin
# (localhost) is skipped even when it is set, so local runs cannot inflate the daily-active-user count.
VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# APP CHECK, optional: the reCAPTCHA ENTERPRISE site key for this site (a "score-based key" created in the Google Cloud
# console - see the README, App Check, for the clicks). Leave it empty and nothing changes: App Check is only
# initialised when a key is present, so switching it on is a deploy rather than a migration. The key is PUBLIC by design,
# like the web config above - it identifies the site, it does not authorise anything by itself.
VITE_FIREBASE_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY=

# DEVELOPMENT ONLY, AND NEVER A REPOSITORY SECRET FOR THE DEPLOYED SITE. `localhost` is not a valid site to attest, so local
# development uses App Check's debug provider: put a debug token from the Firebase console here (Security > App Check > Apps >
# the app's menu > Manage debug tokens) and it will be used instead. Vite INLINES every VITE_* variable into the built
# bundle, so setting this for a Pages build would ship a credential for your own project in the page source. Keep it in your
# local .env only.
#
# Leave it EMPTY and App Check switches itself OFF on a loopback origin (localhost / 127.0.0.1 / ::1), saying so once in the
# console. That is deliberate, not a gap: reCAPTCHA cannot attest a loopback origin, and the SDK has no backoff for that
# failure, so attempting it fills the console with `appCheck/recaptcha-error` on every request until the tab is closed.
# The deployed site is not a loopback origin, so it is unaffected either way.
VITE_FIREBASE_APPCHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN=

# Which routes are on, comma-separated (see src/services/firestoreRouting.js for the names). Leave it UNSET: unset means
# every route whose prerequisites are met, which is the normal state and what a deployment should use. Listing names
# turns on only those, and `off`/`none` is a kill switch for a route that is misbehaving.
#
# It is a KILL SWITCH and nothing else now. There is no sheet to fall back to, so switching a route off means the screens
# that use it fail rather than quietly working from somewhere else - which is the honest behaviour, and worth knowing
# before reaching for it.
VITE_FIRESTORE_FEATURES=
